; La Aventura Original, part one: the word this port added.
;
; QDAAD is no word of 1989. It sits beside AD in table 5 — the easter egg the
; original carries, verb 116 printing the credits — and answers with who
; modified and compiled this adventure and with what.
;
; What the script is for is the text arriving whole. It is the longest message
; either part holds after the two of the prologue, so it goes through the token
; table like the rest, and the blank lines in it are #n and not the 0x0A that
; ends a text. A token pruned wrong or an escape read wrong shows here as a
; text that stops early or runs into the next one, which is why the EXPECTs
; walk it from its first line to its last rather than matching it once.
;
;   qdaad compile -i examples/AO/ZXSpectrum/+3/part1/game.sce -m pc -s vga -f ddb -o tmp/AO1
;   qrun -i tmp/AO1.ddb -T examples/AO/test/part1-qdaad.qtest

TEST "La Aventura Original, parte I: la palabra QDAAD"

SEED 1989

STEP "Pantalla de título y bosque"
  EXPECT "?"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "AVENTURA ORIGINAL"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "LA RESPUESTA DEPENDE DE TU VALOR"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "Estás en el bosque"
  ASSERT AT = 3
END

STEP "QDAAD responde con el aviso del compilador"
  INPUT  QDAAD
  EXPECT "Queru's DAAD Compiler"
  EXPECT "Jorge Fuertes AKA Queru"
  EXPECT "descompilación, compilación y empaquetado de QDAAD"
  EXPECT "software libre en 2026 y disponible en Github."
  EXPECT "¡Gracias por jugar!"

  ; The DONE and nothing else: it answers where the player stands and moves
  ; nobody, which is what keeps it out of the way of the adventure.
  ASSERT AT = 3
END

; And the 1989 word still answers, which is what says the entry was put beside
; it and not over it. Two verbs one after the other in the same table, and the
; parser has to tell them apart.
STEP "Y AD sigue dando los créditos de 1989"
  INPUT  AD
  EXPECT "Tambien participaron"
  ASSERT AT = 3
END
